WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.770
In the previous lesson, your agent could only respond with text.

2
00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:06.473
In this lesson, you'll go beyond text

3
00:00:06.573 --> 00:00:09.476
by building with Controlled Generative UI

4
00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:13.213
which is the workhorse of the generative UI spectrum.

5
00:00:13.513 --> 00:00:16.816
In controlled generative UI, you, as the programmer,

6
00:00:17.017 --> 00:00:19.219
define fully custom components,

7
00:00:19.519 --> 00:00:22.322
which the agent can then use and populate with data.

8
00:00:22.622 --> 00:00:24.924
You the programmer control the look and feel,

9
00:00:25.125 --> 00:00:28.528
and the agent controls the intent. All right, let's get to it.

10
00:00:28.528 --> 00:00:32.932
Going beyond text gives your agent an immediate communication advantage.

11
00:00:33.133 --> 00:00:37.037
For instance, what was previously a long and indecipherable wall of text

12
00:00:37.137 --> 00:00:38.838
can instantly turn into a clear

13
00:00:38.838 --> 00:00:40.840
and concise table or a pie chart.

14
00:00:41.107 --> 00:00:44.310
Controlled Generative UI is the most straightforward

15
00:00:44.344 --> 00:00:46.613
and most widely used variant of Generative UI.

16
00:00:46.913 --> 00:00:49.616
Developers simply define a set of custom components

17
00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:52.318
that the agent is allowed to use in its replies.

18
00:00:52.352 --> 00:00:54.120
along with the arguments that those

19
00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.156
components expect to get from the agent.

20
00:00:56.556 --> 00:01:00.260
The technical setup would be incredibly familiar to any frontend developer

21
00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:04.164
because it mirrors the standard definition of any frontend component.

22
00:01:04.464 --> 00:01:07.367
Since each interaction requires its own component's definition,

23
00:01:07.567 --> 00:01:10.837
the Controlled Generative UI paradigm offers developers maximal flexibility

24
00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:13.073
once the component is displayed.

25
00:01:13.273 --> 00:01:16.476
Components can be interactive and trivially affect other parts of the application.

26
00:01:16.776 --> 00:01:18.511
And they can also be made pixel perfect

27
00:01:18.511 --> 00:01:20.480
to conform to any specs by your design team.

28
00:01:20.747 --> 00:01:22.982
The standard variant of Controlled Generative UI,

29
00:01:22.982 --> 00:01:25.785
which we will explore in this lesson, gives the agent

30
00:01:25.785 --> 00:01:29.255
the ability to decide which UI components to engage with and when.

31
00:01:29.489 --> 00:01:31.691
Controlled Generative UI is considered the workhorse

32
00:01:31.691 --> 00:01:34.394
of the Generative UI universe for a number of reasons.

33
00:01:34.427 --> 00:01:36.329
First, it offers full brand control

34
00:01:36.329 --> 00:01:38.431
because you can fully customize each component

35
00:01:38.431 --> 00:01:40.233
for pixel-perfect designs.

36
00:01:40.233 --> 00:01:42.635
Second, when using Controlled Generative UI,

37
00:01:42.635 --> 00:01:45.839
your agent will never hallucinate a component you've not registered.

38
00:01:46.306 --> 00:01:49.509
At worst, it could call the wrong component at the wrong time

39
00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:52.512
or populate a component with wrong data.

40
00:01:52.912 --> 00:01:54.914
But these are far less likely to happen.

41
00:01:55.115 --> 00:01:57.517
Controlled Generative UI is also the fastest and

42
00:01:57.517 --> 00:01:59.919
most reliable solution along the Generative UI spectrum.

43
00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:02.622
All of these qualities make it the most suitable solution

44
00:02:02.889 --> 00:02:05.692
for your product's most used surfaces.

45
00:02:06.092 --> 00:02:08.394
It's a really great 80/20 solution.

46
00:02:08.695 --> 00:02:11.097
The primary tradeoff of Controlled Generative UI

47
00:02:11.097 --> 00:02:13.700
is that every new capability in your application

48
00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:16.102
requires a new dedicated component definition.

49
00:02:16.302 --> 00:02:17.770
And so complexity of implementation scales

50
00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:19.506
linearly with the scope of the agent.

51
00:02:19.506 --> 00:02:21.908
Here's how Controlled Generative UI works under the hood.

52
00:02:22.208 --> 00:02:24.611
Each registered component is associated with a name,

53
00:02:24.811 --> 00:02:26.913
a description, and a list of arguments

54
00:02:27.013 --> 00:02:30.316
needed to provide by the agent in order to display the component.

55
00:02:30.717 --> 00:02:32.719
These parameters are converted to tool definitions.

56
00:02:33.019 --> 00:02:36.322
The AG-UI middleware then registers these as frontend-defined tools.

57
00:02:36.723 --> 00:02:39.225
When the agent runs, it sees those tools

58
00:02:39.225 --> 00:02:40.827
just like it sees any other tool.

59
00:02:41.127 --> 00:02:43.496
Agent can decide to call those tools

60
00:02:43.496 --> 00:02:45.899
when it wishes to display some content to the user.

61
00:02:46.199 --> 00:02:49.202
When an agent decides to call a frontend tool, the AG-UI connection

62
00:02:49.502 --> 00:02:53.206
halts backend execution and hands off execution to the frontend.

63
00:02:53.506 --> 00:02:57.210
The CopilotKit SDK then maps the frontend tool execution

64
00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:59.712
back to the React component it is associated with,

65
00:03:00.013 --> 00:03:01.714
and displays that component to the user.

66
00:03:02.015 --> 00:03:06.119
Finally, if the tool returns any result upon interactivity, the result is

67
00:03:06.119 --> 00:03:07.854
passed back to the agent back end

68
00:03:07.854 --> 00:03:09.923
as a standard tool call result.

69
00:03:10.223 --> 00:03:13.126
In this lab, you'll learn to use CopilotKit's useComponent hook

70
00:03:13.226 --> 00:03:15.328
register components to the agent.

71
00:03:15.428 --> 00:03:17.297
Without the agent ever being directly exposed

72
00:03:17.297 --> 00:03:19.199
to a React abstraction or to code.

73
00:03:19.399 --> 00:03:21.401
The useComponent hook is straightforward.

74
00:03:21.501 --> 00:03:23.703
Simply give each component a name, a

75
00:03:23.703 --> 00:03:25.905
schema, and a React component or renderer,

76
00:03:26.005 --> 00:03:28.441
and that's it, you're done. You can define components

77
00:03:28.441 --> 00:03:31.110
in a centralized fashion when you initialize your React application,

78
00:03:31.311 --> 00:03:33.246
or you can register components in

79
00:03:33.246 --> 00:03:35.515
different regions and pages in your application.

80
00:03:35.515 --> 00:03:39.018
and these components will become active and available for the agent

81
00:03:39.018 --> 00:03:41.020
as the user traverses your application.

82
00:03:41.321 --> 00:03:43.523
If you're familiar with React hook dynamics,

83
00:03:43.523 --> 00:03:46.326
this hook behaves just like any other React hook,

84
00:03:46.326 --> 00:03:50.029
and all of the backend complexity is handled automatically by the framework.

85
00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:52.999
The useComponent hook explicitly demonstrates

86
00:03:52.999 --> 00:03:55.501
the clean separation of concerns of controlled generative UI.

87
00:03:55.902 --> 00:03:58.705
The agent decides when to display the component

88
00:03:58.705 --> 00:04:00.707
based on its name, description, and schema,

89
00:04:00.707 --> 00:04:03.409
and the programmer decides exactly what the component would look like

90
00:04:03.409 --> 00:04:05.411
and what interaction it would support

91
00:04:05.411 --> 00:04:08.014
by defining a custom React component or renderer.

92
00:04:08.414 --> 00:04:10.817
In this lab, we're going to register three components

93
00:04:10.817 --> 00:04:12.685
with an agent using the useComponent hook.

94
00:04:12.885 --> 00:04:16.189
We'll start with the name component, the simplest possible component registration.

95
00:04:16.289 --> 00:04:19.592
A hello world of sorts to get comfortable with a pattern.

96
00:04:19.692 --> 00:04:21.694
We will then register a pie chart component,

97
00:04:21.794 --> 00:04:23.830
where the agent doesn't just pick the component, it

98
00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:25.898
also figures out what data to fill it with.

99
00:04:25.999 --> 00:04:28.568
Finally, we will register a flight card component

100
00:04:28.668 --> 00:04:31.170
as an example of a pixel-perfect custom design.

101
00:04:31.271 --> 00:04:32.739
By the end of this lab, you

102
00:04:32.739 --> 00:04:34.374
will be able to register any React component

103
00:04:34.474 --> 00:04:36.576
so your agent can render it on demand.

104
00:04:36.676 --> 00:04:40.179
We're going to start by resetting the states of your notebook.

105
00:04:40.179 --> 00:04:42.181
Remember, this is just a helper tool

106
00:04:42.181 --> 00:04:43.883
in case you get into a stuck state,

107
00:04:43.883 --> 00:04:45.985
always worth trying to reset your state.

108
00:04:45.985 --> 00:04:48.655
Just like before, we're going to start by setting up the dependencies.

109
00:04:48.655 --> 00:04:50.456
This section is going to look very familiar.

110
00:04:50.657 --> 00:04:54.160
We're going to start by installing our Python backend dependencies.

111
00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:56.162
And now we're going to load up

112
00:04:56.162 --> 00:04:58.231
our API keys using our helper function.

113
00:04:58.331 --> 00:05:00.733
These are set up for you automatically by the deep learning platform.

114
00:05:00.833 --> 00:05:03.336
Now that we've got everything set up, let's start the agent.

115
00:05:03.636 --> 00:05:05.204
First, we'll start our backend.

116
00:05:05.204 --> 00:05:07.407
It's now running in port 8003.

117
00:05:07.707 --> 00:05:10.009
We'll now use our helper function to start our frontend

118
00:05:10.209 --> 00:05:12.011
in port 3003.

119
00:05:13.513 --> 00:05:15.214
All right. Now that it's running,

120
00:05:15.415 --> 00:05:18.318
we can click into that and play with it. Just like before,

121
00:05:18.351 --> 00:05:20.453
We're going to embed the iframe

122
00:05:20.453 --> 00:05:22.855
of this URL directly in our Jupyter notebook for convenience.

123
00:05:23.556 --> 00:05:25.858
using the display_app helper function.

124
00:05:26.559 --> 00:05:28.361
Now we've got the basic front end

125
00:05:28.361 --> 00:05:30.763
that we set up in lesson two all set

126
00:05:30.763 --> 00:05:33.166
up for us. All right, the chat is working.

127
00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:36.803
We're now going to venture outside of pure text chat

128
00:05:36.803 --> 00:05:38.805
with Controlled Generative UI.

129
00:05:39.205 --> 00:05:41.307
Remember, in Controlled Generative UI,

130
00:05:41.507 --> 00:05:44.110
we as programmers register components

131
00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:47.113
that the agent can use when it chooses to.

132
00:05:47.213 --> 00:05:50.316
We're going to start by registering these components with the agent.

133
00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:55.550
In this, we've added three useComponent hook calls to our application code.

134
00:05:56.055 --> 00:05:57.757
Registering three different components that are now

135
00:05:57.757 --> 00:05:59.459
available for the agent to call upon

136
00:05:59.559 --> 00:06:02.662
as part of its controlled generative UI toolbox.

137
00:06:03.062 --> 00:06:05.064
Let's go through each of them one by one.

138
00:06:05.064 --> 00:06:07.367
The useComponent hook takes four different arguments.

139
00:06:07.734 --> 00:06:09.335
The first argument is the name

140
00:06:09.369 --> 00:06:10.937
argument. This is the name of

141
00:06:10.937 --> 00:06:12.772
the component made available to the agent.

142
00:06:13.072 --> 00:06:15.274
The next argument is the description argument.

143
00:06:15.575 --> 00:06:17.510
This is a simple string, but it's very important

144
00:06:17.977 --> 00:06:20.346
because it tells the agent when and how

145
00:06:20.847 --> 00:06:22.849
to use this given component.

146
00:06:23.449 --> 00:06:24.951
In this case, we're telling the agent

147
00:06:24.951 --> 00:06:26.652
to show the user's name in a card.

148
00:06:26.853 --> 00:06:28.654
Just a short sentence here. But if

149
00:06:28.654 --> 00:06:30.690
you find that the agent does not display

150
00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:33.860
your component at times when you expect it to,

151
00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:37.864
you can continue to add this description to your heart's content.

152
00:06:38.197 --> 00:06:41.667
Some applications even have dozens of lines in this description,

153
00:06:42.168 --> 00:06:43.936
teasing out a very specific scenario in

154
00:06:43.936 --> 00:06:45.671
which the given component should be shown.

155
00:06:45.872 --> 00:06:49.375
The next argument is the parameters that this given component expects

156
00:06:49.675 --> 00:06:52.979
to get from the agent when it is called upon.

157
00:06:53.379 --> 00:06:55.047
This component, the showMyName component, is

158
00:06:55.047 --> 00:06:56.983
the simplest component we can think of.

159
00:06:57.283 --> 00:06:59.986
So there is really just a single argument it expects,

160
00:07:00.186 --> 00:07:04.157
which is the name of the person, its type is a string.

161
00:07:04.157 --> 00:07:06.259
We're using "zod" to specify the

162
00:07:06.259 --> 00:07:08.761
schema of arguments this component is expecting.

163
00:07:08.761 --> 00:07:12.765
The schema consists of both argument names and argument types.

164
00:07:12.765 --> 00:07:14.600
In this case, the argument name is

165
00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:16.436
name, and the argument type is string.

166
00:07:16.436 --> 00:07:19.739
Zod is the TypeScript equivalent of Pydantic.

167
00:07:19.739 --> 00:07:22.742
The next argument is the actual renderer for this component.

168
00:07:22.742 --> 00:07:26.946
It's a function that takes the arguments we specified in the parameters

169
00:07:26.946 --> 00:07:31.451
and maps it to a simple React component. In this case,

170
00:07:31.818 --> 00:07:35.688
we're mapping the name argument that we specified previously

171
00:07:35.988 --> 00:07:39.792
to a very simple inline React component that displays this name.

172
00:07:40.293 --> 00:07:42.161
Now let's look at the next component.

173
00:07:42.161 --> 00:07:45.665
This next component is a PieChart component.

174
00:07:46.065 --> 00:07:48.968
And the description of this component tells the agent

175
00:07:48.968 --> 00:07:51.137
that it can use this component to display data

176
00:07:51.137 --> 00:07:53.239
in the form of a pie chart.

177
00:07:53.439 --> 00:07:56.642
The agent also sees the types of this component,

178
00:07:56.642 --> 00:07:58.644
it can infer from those types

179
00:07:58.845 --> 00:08:01.647
when and how that component should be used.

180
00:08:02.048 --> 00:08:03.716
In this case, we're importing the

181
00:08:03.716 --> 00:08:05.651
props of a pre-built pie chart component.

182
00:08:05.852 --> 00:08:08.855
These are standard React component props,

183
00:08:09.055 --> 00:08:10.690
and if you'd like, you can go

184
00:08:10.690 --> 00:08:12.358
and explore them to your heart's content.

185
00:08:12.558 --> 00:08:16.863
Finally, the render function in this case is taking a React component

186
00:08:17.063 --> 00:08:19.632
as opposed to specifying a function in line.

187
00:08:19.832 --> 00:08:21.834
A React component is itself

188
00:08:22.101 --> 00:08:25.004
a function which takes arguments of the props

189
00:08:25.004 --> 00:08:27.907
and returns a rendered React component.

190
00:08:28.307 --> 00:08:31.310
Lastly, we're registering a flightCard component to show flights to the user.

191
00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:32.845
You'll also notice that we've added

192
00:08:32.845 --> 00:08:34.614
another hook call to our app code,

193
00:08:34.614 --> 00:08:38.784
useExampleSuggestions. This is going to populate our chat

194
00:08:38.784 --> 00:08:41.787
with pre-built suggestions that we can click on

195
00:08:41.787 --> 00:08:43.189
so that we don't have to

196
00:08:43.189 --> 00:08:44.790
remember what to type into the prompt.

197
00:08:44.891 --> 00:08:46.559
And lastly, you will see we

198
00:08:46.559 --> 00:08:48.494
still have our CopilotChat component down here.

199
00:08:49.095 --> 00:08:51.497
Once again, you could customize this component,

200
00:08:51.497 --> 00:08:54.100
custom CSS or custom subcomponents

201
00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:57.303
or build your own fully custom chat UI from scratch

202
00:08:57.503 --> 00:09:00.106
using the headless useAgent hook.

203
00:09:00.473 --> 00:09:02.108
If you want to explore the source

204
00:09:02.108 --> 00:09:04.110
code of the PieChart and the FlightCard components,

205
00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:06.812
you can click into the frontend folder and under components

206
00:09:06.913 --> 00:09:08.614
you'll find both of these components laid out.

207
00:09:08.814 --> 00:09:12.018
These are standard React components, there's nothing special there.

208
00:09:12.018 --> 00:09:14.120
Now we're ready to display the app once again.

209
00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:16.255
We'll once again embed the iframe

210
00:09:16.255 --> 00:09:17.924
right here in the Jupyter for convenience.

211
00:09:18.324 --> 00:09:20.126
Now we have three pre-built suggestions here.

212
00:09:20.126 --> 00:09:21.894
When we press one of the suggestions,

213
00:09:21.994 --> 00:09:24.897
it's going to populate our text with a pre-built prompt.

214
00:09:25.298 --> 00:09:27.099
Let's try the first one, Show my name.

215
00:09:27.166 --> 00:09:29.268
You see that the chat added a new prompt.

216
00:09:29.268 --> 00:09:31.370
Please show my name in the chat.

217
00:09:31.370 --> 00:09:33.472
The agent does not yet know my name,

218
00:09:33.472 --> 00:09:35.274
so it's asking me, hey, what is your name?

219
00:09:35.274 --> 00:09:37.376
so I can display for you. Well, my name is Atai.

220
00:09:37.977 --> 00:09:40.947
Now it said my name. My name is appearing in blue background

221
00:09:40.947 --> 00:09:43.749
because it's no longer replying in text.

222
00:09:43.749 --> 00:09:48.321
It's using the show my name component we defined earlier, which

223
00:09:48.321 --> 00:09:51.223
shows my name in a text wrapped in a blue rectangle.

224
00:09:51.724 --> 00:09:53.726
Now let's move on to the next component.

225
00:09:54.126 --> 00:09:56.128
Uh, Pie chart.

226
00:09:56.228 --> 00:09:58.230
You see here when we click this suggestion, there's just a pre-canned

227
00:09:58.264 --> 00:09:59.865
text that goes in there.

228
00:10:00.266 --> 00:10:03.269
And the agent replied with a fully featured component

229
00:10:03.569 --> 00:10:06.072
that we rendered and controlled from end to end.

230
00:10:06.572 --> 00:10:08.341
Isn't that nice? Lastly, let's check

231
00:10:08.341 --> 00:10:10.376
out the last suggestion, the flight card.

232
00:10:10.476 --> 00:10:12.678
And we see here a flight component fully rendered.

233
00:10:13.079 --> 00:10:16.682
As you see here, these are just simple, straightforward React components.

234
00:10:17.083 --> 00:10:19.585
And so you can fully customize them to your heart's content

235
00:10:20.086 --> 00:10:22.388
and even make them fully interactive, including

236
00:10:22.788 --> 00:10:24.690
with other parts of your application. That's it.

237
00:10:24.890 --> 00:10:26.792
You've now mastered Controlled Generative UI,

238
00:10:27.159 --> 00:10:30.062
the workhorse of a Generative UI universe.
